// Cantor pairing N x N -> N as a clean reversible I/O program.
//
// Reads x and y, writes their pairing z = (x+y)(x+y+1)/2 + y, then echoes x and
// y. z is computed with a pure expression and `+=` (no reversible `/=` needed),
// leaving x,y intact; echoing them makes the run reversible end to end (the full
// unpair from z alone needs a triangular-root loop, see the no-I/O twin under
// tests/jana2014/fixtures/examples/ and docs/reversible-pearls.md). Backward
// consumes the output stream in reverse and reproduces the input.
//
// Distinct (x,y) give distinct z (injective): (1,2)=8 but (2,1)=7.
//
// case: origin
// in: 0 0
// out: 0 0 0
// case: row
// in: 1 2
// out: 8 1 2
// case: col
// in: 2 1
// out: 7 2 1
// case: bigger
// in: 3 5
// out: 41 3 5
procedure pair(int x, int y, int z)
    z += (x + y) * (x + y + 1) / 2 + y

procedure main()
    int x
    int y
    int z
    read x
    read y
    call pair(x, y, z)
    write z
    write x
    write y
